{"name":"Mercedes-Benz 540 K (W 29)","title":"Under the skin of the 540 K","lede":"Three things about the supercharged 540 K, each shown working: the independent front suspension meeting a bump and a pothole, the Roots blower that only joins in when the driver floors the pedal, and what a streamlined body does for top speed. Scroll on: the museum photo then shows the suspension in place on the real car.","components":[["Front suspension",["Most cars of the 1930s still had a rigid beam axle on leaf springs at the front. Mercedes gave the 380 of 1933 parallel wishbones and coil springs, and the 500 K and 540 K kept them.","Each front wheel now moves on its own: a bump under one wheel no longer tilts the other. Watch the orange trace spike over the bump and fall away in the pothole.","With near-parallel arms the wheel stays almost upright, but the tyre slides sideways a few millimetres as it rises. See the track change readout."]],["Supercharger",["K stands for Kompressor. The 5.4-litre straight-eight makes 115 PS on its own and 180 PS with the blower working.","The blower is not always on. Push the pedal through to the floor and a multi-plate clutch on the nose of the crankshaft couples it in; the engine's normal air inlet closes at the same moment. It was meant for short bursts.","Mercedes blew the air through the carburettor instead of sucking mixture through the blower. A Roots blower does not squeeze air inside itself: the lobes carry pockets of air round the outside of the casing and push them into the outlet."]],["Air drag",["In 1938 Mercedes built one 540 K with a closed, streamlined body so that Dunlop could test tyres at sustained high speed on the new autobahns.","Mercedes-Benz Classic measured the restored car in the wind tunnel in May 2014: a drag coefficient of 0.36, remarkable for its time.","With the same 180 PS it reaches about 185 km/h, against about 170 km/h for an open Cabriolet. Drag rises with the square of speed, so those 15 km/h take a lot of power: slide it and see.","In the wind tunnel, compare the two bodies. Behind the Cabriolet's upright windscreen and roof the air breaks away early into a wide wake; along the Streamliner's long, sloping tail it stays on the body for longer and leaves a smaller wake."]]],"note":"Geometry is illustrative: suspension hardpoints, blower layout, drive ratio, boost and spring rates are plausible values, not factory data."}
