{"name":"Mercedes-Benz 300 SL","title":"Why the doors open upwards","lede":"Twist the 300 SL's tube frame as if one front wheel rode up a kerb, then cut its sides down for ordinary doors and twist it again. Then turn over its straight-six, laid over on its side, with fuel sprayed straight into the cylinders.","components":[["Space frame",["Rudolf Uhlenhaut's engineers built the 1952 racing 300 SL around a frame of thin welded steel tubes, arranged in triangles so that each tube is only pulled or pushed, never bent. The road car of 1954 kept it.","Red tubes are in tension, blue in compression. The sides stay stiff only because their triangles run deep, right up through where a door would be.","So the doors are hinged in the roof and open upwards: the Gullwing. Getting in still means climbing over a high, wide sill.","Cut the sides down to a low sill and the same frame twists about three times as much under the same load (in this simple model)."]],["Engine",["The M 198 is the 3-litre six of the 300 saloon, reworked: 2,996 cm³, 215 PS (DIN) at 5,800 rpm, 240 hp by the American SAE rating.","A Bosch pump sprays petrol straight into each cylinder during the intake stroke: the first four-stroke production car with petrol direct injection. Watch for the spray.","The engine leans 45° to the left so that the bonnet could sit low. A dry sump, with the oil in a separate tank, keeps the crankcase shallow."]]],"note":"1,400 coupés were built from 1954 to 1957. The idea for a road version came from the American importer Max Hoffman."}
