{"name":"Mercedes-Benz Unimog U 500","title":"Axles above the wheels","lede":"A Unimog's axle does not run through the middle of its wheels but above them: each hub has a pair of gears that drops the drive down to the wheel. That lifts the axle tube and differential out of the way. Drive over a rock and switch to a conventional axle to compare.","components":[["Portal axle",["Unimog stands for Universal-Motor-Gerät, a universal motorised implement: designed after the war as a small tractor that could also drive fast on the road, and built by Daimler-Benz since 1951.","Its axles are portal axles. The half-shafts run above the wheel centres and drive each wheel through a pair of spur gears in the hub, so the axle tube and the differential sit higher by the gears' drop, leaving more room underneath.","The hub gears also reduce: the shafts turn faster than the wheels and carry less torque, so the shafts and the differential can be lighter.","Coil springs and a ladder frame that is allowed to twist let each wheel follow the ground. This U 500 is fitted out for winter service."]]],"note":"Axle and housing sizes are round figures chosen to show the principle."}
