{"name":"Mercedes-Benz ESF 22","title":"Brakes that let go","lede":"Brake hard enough and a wheel stops turning: the tyre slides, grips less, and no longer steers. The anti-lock system on this test car lets the brake off just before that happens, then on again, many times a second. Brake from 100 km/h and compare the two stops.","components":[["Anti-lock brakes",["A tyre grips best when it turns a little slower than the road passes under it: about 10 to 20 % slip. A locked wheel slides at 100 % slip, with less grip, and it can no longer take the side force that steers the car.","The anti-lock system watches each wheel's speed. When one slows too quickly, it is about to lock: a valve holds back the brake pressure until the wheel speeds up again, then lets it build once more.","The cycle repeats several times a second, keeping the tyre near its best grip. The stop is shorter on dry and wet roads, and the driver can still steer round an obstacle.","Mercedes-Benz and Bosch worked on anti-lock brakes through the 1970s. Test cars like this ESF 22 carried them years before the system went on sale in the S-Class in 1978."]]],"note":"ESF stands for Experimental-Sicherheits-Fahrzeug, experimental safety vehicle: Mercedes-Benz built a series of them in the early 1970s for an international safety programme."}
