{"name":"Mercedes-Benz SLK","title":"A roof in the boot","lede":"Press a button and the steel roof unlatches, the boot lid swings up, and roof and rear window fold back into the boot as one pack before the lid closes over them. Watch the sequence from the side.","components":[["Vario-roof",["Folding steel hardtops had been tried before, but the SLK of 1996 brought one to a series-production car: closed, it is a coupé with a fixed roof's quiet and security; open, a roadster.","The roof is two pieces: the panel over the seats and the rear-window section. Opening, hydraulic cylinders swing the boot lid up about its rear hinge, then turn the rear section backwards and down, carrying the roof, which folds onto it.","The pack ends up stacked in the boot, and the lid closes over it. With the roof down, less luggage space is left.","The whole sequence takes about 25 seconds, driven by a hydraulic pump and switched step by step by sensors at each position."]]],"note":"No freely licensed photo of the SLK in the museum was available; this photo shows a 1997–2000 SLK photographed in a street in Washington, D.C."}
